# Golden dataset for {{service}} ({{brand_title}}). THREE worked examples.
#
# Read README.md in this directory first: it documents the assert vocabulary, the
# fixed evaluation clock, and the rule that a case is never edited to make a run
# pass.
#
# =============================================================================
# THIS PACK DOES NOT PASS THE GATE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
# =============================================================================
#
# Every case below carries `provenance.author: pave-template`. `pave verify`
# counts only cases a seat has DISPOSED — the ones whose author is not the
# template — so this pack counts as **zero** against a floor of twenty, and the
# refusal says so by name.
#
# Flipping `author` without reading the case is a lie the repository cannot
# catch, and it is worth knowing that up front rather than discovering it: the
# floor counts disposition, and disposition is a claim you are making. What the
# repository can tell you is what these three examples are for.
#
# Three, not twenty, deliberately. Twenty scaffolded rows is an invitation to
# edit the author field twenty times. Three is enough to show the vocabulary and
# too few to mistake for a pack.
#
# =============================================================================
# WHAT YOU MAY CITE
# =============================================================================
#
# `data/catalog.json` holds five fictional titles. Three carry
# `brand: meridian-sports` — t001, t003, t005 — and the examples below use them.
#
# **A service that needs its own catalog content is blocked today, for any
# brand.** The catalog is embedded model-facing in the judge prompt and digested
# into `quality/judge/frozen.json`, so adding one title is 16 failed and runs
# through a judge re-freeze (two-key `ai-quality`) plus superseding history
# entries. That is a real constraint on what you can write here, not a hint to
# work around, and it is recorded in ADR-047.
#
# Owning seat: AI Quality. Two-key on every change, including this file's first
# real content.

# =============================================================================
# THE BUDGET NUMBERS BELOW ARE FOR ONE TOOL
# =============================================================================
#
# `budget: { ... tokens_in: 6000 ... }` in case 1 is a PER-TURN ceiling, and a
# tool loop makes a turn n model calls — every round after the first re-sends the
# transcript so far (ADR-014 amendment 1). The scaffold declares one tool, so
# 6000 is a number for the shape it ships with and for no other.
#
# Declare a second tool in `pave.manifest.yaml` and this number is wrong, along
# with that file's `max_tokens_in: 6500` and `p95_ms: 2500`. Measured on the
# reference service at three tools: 6000 was re-derived to 7700 (ADR-014
# amendment 2) and 2500 to 5200 (amendment 3), both by a rule written before the
# run it was applied to and pinned in `tests/test_budget_derivation.py`. Derive
# yours the same way. Do not raise one to make a run pass — CLAUDE.md's "never
# edit a golden case to make a run pass" is the same rule one file over.
#
# `pave verify` catches exactly one of the three (`max_tokens_in`, row 15) and
# names `p95_ms` in its "not checked" footer. This case's own `tokens_in` it does
# not check at all: a disposed case's budget is AI Quality's, and a shape check
# has no population to derive one from.

# --- 1. deterministic asserts only, and this is the shape to prefer ----------
# CLAUDE.md: "Prefer deterministic assertions over judge assertions wherever a
# deterministic one can express the requirement." A case with no `judge:` block
# is cheaper, faster and cannot drift when a judge is re-calibrated.
- id: example-001-deterministic
  input: "What replays can I watch right now?"
  viewer: { plan: base, dma: north-haven }
  fixtures: [data/catalog.json]
  asserts:
    - json_schema: services/{{service}}/evals/answer.schema.json
    - must_cite: [t003]
    - cited_titles_in_fixture: true
    # tokens_in 6000 is a ONE-TOOL number — see the block above before you add a
    # second tool to `pave.manifest.yaml`.
    - budget: { model: haiku, tokens_in: 6000, tokens_out: 300, max_ms: 12000 }
  provenance:
    author: pave-template
    note: >-
      Replace this with a request your service actually receives, and delete
      this note. `cited_titles_in_fixture` is what makes groundedness
      deterministic: a title id absent from the catalog is a confabulation and
      no judge is needed to say so.

# --- 2. a judged case, for the requirement no assert can express -------------
- id: example-002-judged
  input: "Tell me about the derby this weekend."
  viewer: { plan: sports-tier, dma: north-haven }
  fixtures: [data/catalog.json]
  asserts:
    - json_schema: services/{{service}}/evals/answer.schema.json
    - must_cite: [t001]
    - cited_titles_in_fixture: true
  judge:
    axes: [groundedness, completeness, brand_tone:{{brand}}]
    rubric: quality/judge/rubric-sports.md
  provenance:
    author: pave-template
    note: >-
      Add a `judge:` block only for what a deterministic assert cannot state —
      tone, completeness, whether an answer is actually responsive. The axis
      `brand_tone:{{brand}}` must exist in the rubric or the judge raises; that
      is why `--brand` accepts one value today.

# --- 3. the headroom case, and why the pack needs some -----------------------
# CLAUDE.md: "Keep headroom: 5-10% of cases at or near failure. A suite at 100%
# can only report 'no change or regression' — improvements become invisible."
# `pave verify` enforces that band over your disposed cases, so a pack of twenty
# needs one or two cases marked here. Mark cases a CORRECT answer only just
# passes — never cases that are broken.
- id: example-003-headroom
  input: "Is the rowing final on before the derby?"
  viewer: { plan: sports-tier, dma: cedar-point }
  fixtures: [data/catalog.json]
  asserts:
    - json_schema: services/{{service}}/evals/answer.schema.json
    - must_cite: [t001, t005]
    - cited_titles_in_fixture: true
  expect_near_threshold: true
  provenance:
    author: pave-template
    note: >-
      `expect_near_threshold` is a TOP-LEVEL key. It used to live under `judge:`,
      which meant a headroom case needed a judge block invoking no judge; both
      locations are not accepted, because a typo in the nested one is caught by
      nothing at a pack size of twenty (ADR-045).
